The Jesus Family Series
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob. Jacob was the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah is the father of "Perez" and "Zerah", and Tamar was their mother. "Perez" was the father of "Hezron", and "Hezron" was the father of "Ram".
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Samson was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of king David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Ahaziah.
Uzziah was the father of Jotham. Jotham was the father of Ahaz. Ahaz was the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Menahem. Menahem was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Jehoiakim.
And Josiah was the father of Jehoiakim and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to migrate to Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Kiyiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zorobabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azur was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Akim. Akim was the father of Eliud.
Elioenai was the father of Elizur. Elizur was the father of Matan. Matan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph, and Joseph was the husband of Mary, and Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David until the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was promised in marriage to Joseph. But before they came together, it became clear that she would conceive a child through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Yusuf, was a devout man. He did not wish to publicly shame her. Therefore, he planned to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will have a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All of this happened to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son. She will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God is with us."
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him to do. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have intimate relations with her until after she gave birth to a son. And Joseph called his name Jesus.
The Visit of the Wise Men to Jesus
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place during the time when Herod was king over Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed. Everyone in Jerusalem was also perplexed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and scribes of the people. He asked them where the Messiah was to be born. "In Bethlehem in Judea," they replied. "For this
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written through the prophet:
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come one who will be a ruler of Israel, whose origin is from old, from ancient times." (Micah 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He discovered from them exactly when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem, and he said, "Go and search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, come and tell me. After that, I can also go to worship him."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they continued on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another road. Jesus' family flees to Egypt
When the wise men had gone, Joseph dreamed. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Rise!" "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt." "Stay there until I tell you to return." "Herod will look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Then the word of the Lord, which he spoke through the prophet, came true: "I called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the young boys who were two years old and under were to be killed. This corresponds to the time when the wise men had seen the star.
In this way the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of weeping and great mourning; Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph had a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who tried to kill the child have died."
Then Joseph arose and took the child and his mother Mary and returned with them to the land of Israel.
But then he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. Joseph was afraid to go there. After being warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. And he believed what the prophets had said about Jesus. They had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Turn away from your sins! The kingdom of heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel hair. He had a belt of skin around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people came to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, and from all the regions around the Jordan.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees approaching the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath of God?"
Bear fruits indicating your turning away from your sins.
Do not suppose that you are saying to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe (al-mijannah) has struck the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down. It will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, I call you to repent of your sins. But after me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
I got bored in his hand to refine the straw from his harvested field. He will gather his wheat in the storage room. But the chaff will be burned with unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. He told Jesus, "I need you to baptize me. So why have you come to me?"
Jesus replied, "Let it be so now. It is proper for us to do this. It is the fulfilling of the divine plan of God." Then John agreed.
After Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. At that moment, the heavens were opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descend on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; I am very pleased with him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. There, the devil tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
The tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Exodus 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He stood him on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus replied to him, saying, "It is written again, 'Do not put the Lord your God to the test.' " (Exodus 16:6)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their splendor.
"If you bow down to me and worship me," he said, "I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.'" (Exodus 13:6)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took charge of him. Jesus began to preach.
John was put in prison. When Jesus heard about it, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was located on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, the prophecy of the prophet Isaiah was fulfilled. He said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee where non-Jews live! Land on the Mediterranean coast! Territory east of the Jordan River!
People who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them. " (Isaiah 9:1,2)
From that time Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
One day, Jesus was walking by the Lake of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus traveled throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He proclaimed the good news about the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and weakness the people had.
His news spread throughout all of Syria. People brought to him everyone suffering from various diseases. Some were suffering from intense pain. And others were under the control of demons. And some were trembling intensely. And others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
A great multitude followed him. Some people came from Galilee and from the region known as the Decapolis and from Jerusalem and Judea. And others came from the region beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are those who spiritually need, for the Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessings are for those who are free from pride; the earth will be given to them.
Grace is for those who hunger and yearn for the truth, they will be satisfied.
Blessing is for whoever shows mercy, mercy will be returned to him.
Blessings are for those whose hearts are pure. They will perceive God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are they who suffer for doing right. The kingdom of heaven is theirs.
«Blessed are you when men revile you and persecute you falsely for my sake. And blessed are you also when they revile and persecute you for my sake, spreading all kinds of malicious falsehoods about you for my sake.»
Be happy and joyful. Your reward in paradise is great. In the same way, people harmed the prophets who lived a long time ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. It will be trampled underfoot by people.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket. Instead, they put it on a stand, where it gives light to everyone in the house.
Neither do people light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. And it illuminates everyone in the house.
Likewise, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will give praise to your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the law its full meaning.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the law or the prophets; I have not come to do that, but I have come to give complete meaning to what is written.
What I tell you is truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an smallest stroke of a pen in the law shall pass away, till all be fulfilled.
Do not even violate the least important commandment. And do not teach others how to violate it. If you do, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. Instead, practice and teach these commandments. Then you will be called the important person in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
I heard what was said to the people long ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13). Everyone who murders will be held accountable for their action.
But here is what I tell you. Do not be angry with your brother. Whoever is angry with his brother will be brought to judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, "Raca," must be presented to the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, "You fool!" will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Imagine that you are offering your gift at the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled to your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. You will not leave until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
I heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery" (Exodus 20:14).
Here's what I tell you. Don't even give a wrong look to a woman. Whoever does that has already committed adultery in his heart with her.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand is causing you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is nothing more than a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "If a man separates from his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Exodus 24:1)
But here is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man does not have the right to divorce his wife unless she has been unfaithful to him. The Right Wing
Once again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the covenants you have made with God. Keep the oath you have sworn to Him."
But here's what I'm telling you. Don't make any such promise at all. Don't give it in heaven's name. It's God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the earth; it is the footstool of God's feet. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem; it is the city of the Great King.
Do not swear by your head. You cannot make a single hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes". And let "no" mean "no". Anything else comes from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I tell you: Do not resist an evil person. "If someone strikes you on your right cheek, offer him your left cheek as well.
Imagine someone taking you to court to get your shirt. Let your coat also have it.
Imagine someone is forcing you to walk one mile. Walk two miles with them.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not reject the one who wants to lend you something. Love your enemies.
I heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor" (Lev 19:18) and hate your enemy.
But here's what I'm telling you: Love your enemies. Pray for those who mistreat you.
Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the just and the unjust.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collection does that.
If you are only greeting your own people, what more are you doing than others? Even unbelievers do that.
Let you be perfect, as your Father in heaven is perfect.
Providing assistance to those in need.
Beware not to do "good works" in front of others. Do not do them to be seen by others. If you do, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the poor, do not announce it with a trumpet. Do not be like those who disguise themselves in the garb of sanctity. They proclaim what they do in the temples and on the pavements. They want to be honoured by others. What I am going to tell you is the truth. They have had their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your giving will be secret. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the temples and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who cannot be seen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not go on babbling like the pagans do. They think that they will be heard because of their many words.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need even before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
O King of the Worlds, enjoy your kingdom. And you who wish that it may be, let it be as in heaven, so also on earth.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Protect us from falling into sin when we are tempted. Save us from Satan.
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you go without eating, do not look gloomy like those who only pretend to be devout. They make their faces very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am going to tell you is the truth. They have received their full reward.
But when you go away hungry, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then others will not know that you are fasting. Only your father who does not see will know. He will reward you. Your father sees what is done secretly. Keep your treasures in heaven.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up your treasures in heaven. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. If your eyes are healthy, your whole body will be filled with light.
But what if your eyes are bad? Darkness will engulf your entire body. If the light within you is darkness, it means the darkness is extremely intense!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either you will hate one and love the other, or you will be devoted to one and despise the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Don't worry.
Let me tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, what you will wear. Is there nothing more than eating in life? Are there not things more important for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky, they do not sow nor do they gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not worth much more than they are?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you worry about clothing? Look at how the wild flowers grow. They do not spin, nor do they weave clothing.
But here is what I say to you. Even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of those flowers.
If God clothes the wild grass like this, will He not clothe you even better? After all, the grass is only here today. Tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is very weak!
Therefore, do not worry. Do not say, "What will we eat?" or "What will we drink?" or "What will we wear?"
People who are not believers aspire after all those things. Your father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God and his righteousness. Do as he would have you do. And all these things will be given you also.
Therefore, do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will take care of itself. Each day has enough of its own troubles.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, for then no one will judge you.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You look at the piece of wood in your friend's eye, but you do not notice the piece of wood in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend, "Let me remove the piece of wood from your eye"? How do you say this while there is a piece of wood in your eye?
O fake one! First, remove the wooden beam from your eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to remove the wooden splinter from your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before swine. If you do, they may trample them under their feet. Then they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and it will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks receives, and everyone who seeks finds, and the door will be opened for whoever knocks.
What if your son asked for bread? Some of you would give him a stone?
Or imagine if he asked for a fish. Which one of you would give him a snake?
Although you are a sinner, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your heavenly Father give good gifts to those who ask Him!
In everything, treat others as you would like to be treated. This is what was written in the Law and in the Prophets. The Great Gate and the Small Gate.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to destruction and hell. Many people walk down this road.
But the gate is small and the way is narrow that leads to life. Only a few people find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You will recognize them by their deeds. "Do men gather grapes from thornbushes or figs from thistles?"
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not produce corrupt fruits, and a corrupt tree does not produce good fruits.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
You can distinguish every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the person who does the will of my Father in heaven will enter.
Many will say to me on that day, 'Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name and drive out demons in your name and perform many miracles in your name?'
Then I will clearly say to them: "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise and foolish builders.
Therefore, everyone who hears these words of mine and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain is falling. The waters rise. The winds blow and shake that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them will be like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The rain pours. The waters rise. The winds blow and buffet that house. And it falls with a loud sound like thunder.
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowd was amazed at his teachings.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as their teachers of the law.
Jesus heals a man with a skin disease.
Jesus descended from the mountainside. Great crowds followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus, saying, "Lord, if you are willing to make me clean, it is within your power."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "Be 'clean'!" Immediately the man was healed from his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. You will be a witness to them." A Roman Centurion Believes
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
"Sir," he said, "my servant is lying down in the house and he cannot move. He is suffering severely."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy to have you come under my roof. But if you say the word, my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to one, 'Go,' and he goes. And I say to another, 'Come,' and he comes. And I say to my servant, 'Do this,' and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. And he said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this.
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will eat with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think that they are the people of the Kingdom, they will be cast out into the outer darkness. There they will be howling and gnashing their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman centurion: "Go! It will be done as you have believed." And at that exact hour his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down in bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her body. She got up and began to serve him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many who were possessed by demons. He cast out the spirits with a word. And he healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He bore the pain that we should have borne. And He carried the diseases that should have been ours" (Isaiah 53:4). The Cost of Following Jesus
Jesus saw the crowds around him, so he instructed his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus replied, "The foxes have dens and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
Another follower said to him: "Sir, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calms the storm.
Jesus entered the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly a terrible storm blew over the lake. Huge waves swept over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Oh Lord! Save us! We are going to drown!"
He answered and said, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus arose and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they became perfectly calm. There was a great calm.
The students were astonished and asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men possessed by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of Gerasenes. And a man from the city, in whom demons had entered, who had broken the chains and was kept under guard, but had broken free
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you not come here to punish us before the time of our reckoning?"
Not far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons asked Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go." So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hillside. They surged into the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were serving the pigs fled. They entered the city and said, "All is lost." They told the people what had happened to the men who were under the control of demons.
Then all the city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged that he would depart from their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and arrived at his own town.
Some men brought to Him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Seeing their faith, Jesus said to the man, "Do not be afraid, son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the scholars of the law said to themselves, "This man says something that is very wicked!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. And he said, "Why do you have evil thoughts in your hearts?
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven," or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. He said to him, "Get up, take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowds saw this, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
As Jesus was leaving there, he saw a man named Matthew. He was sitting at the tax booth. "Follow me," Jesus said. So Matthew got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus sat down to eat at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and sinners came. They ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard this, and said, "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but the sick do."
Go and learn what it means to say, 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice' (Hosea 6:6). I did not come for those who believe they are right with God, but I came for sinners." "Jesus asks about fasting"
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast and do not eat. Why then do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus said, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto an old garment. The new piece would tear away from the old. This would make the hole even worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins; otherwise, the wineskins burst, the wine spills out, and the wineskins are ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. Thus, both wineskins are preserved. (A dead girl and a suffering woman)
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. "My daughter has just died," he said. "But come and put your hand on her, and she will be alive again."
Jesus rose up and walked with him. And his disciples did likewise.
At that moment, a woman approached Jesus from behind. She had been suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed, and it had been with her for 12 years. She touched the edge of his robe.
"I thought, 'All I need to do is touch his clothes. Then I will be cured.'"
Jesus turned and saw her. "Woman, you have great faith," he said. "Your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed at that moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Depart. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowds went out, Jesus entered. He took the girl's hand, and she arose.
The news about what Jesus had done spread throughout the whole region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And Jesus was already gone from there, and two blind men followed him. And they cried out, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David."
When Jesus entered in, those who were blind came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" And they answered, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes. And he said, "It shall be to you as you have believed."
They were now able to see again. Jesus strongly warned them, saying, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere in that area.
While they were going out, another man was brought to Jesus. An evil spirit controlled him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowd was astonished. They said, "Such a thing has never been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few laborers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, and preached the gospel of the kingdom. And he healed all manner of sickness and disease.
When he saw the crowds, he felt deep concern for them. They were defeated and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is great, but the laborers are few.
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus Sends Out the Twelve Disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and every affliction.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. First, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
And today Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who later delivered Jesus into the hands of his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with this instruction: "Don't go among the Gentiles," he said. "Don't enter any Samaritan town."
Instead, go to the people of Israel. They are like sheep that have gone astray.
As you go, proclaim this message: 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near.'
Healing the sick. Raising the dead. Giving health to those suffering from skin diseases. Casting out demons. I received freely, so I give freely.
You shall not carry with you gold or silver or copper in your purses.
Do not take a travel bag. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker must be provided with what they need.
When you enter a city or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at this person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, you greet those who live in it.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with peace. Otherwise, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to what you say. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or that town.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. The Day of Judgment will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out as sheep among wolves. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Beware! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their synagogue.
You will be led before rulers and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for the non-Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
It will not be you who speaks, but the spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver their brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But the one who endures to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
A student is no better than his teacher. A servant is no better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher, and it is enough for the servant to be like his master. If the head of the household is called Beelzebul, what will the others who live there expect?
Fear not your enemies. What is hidden will be revealed. What is concealed will be made known.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light of day. What was whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Fear not those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul. Rather, fear the one who is able to destroy both body and soul in hell.
Is it not sold, two sparrows for one farthing? But the one of them does not fall on the ground without your father knowing about it.
Until he counts every hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to ignite children against their parents. Daughters will refuse to obey their mothers. Daughters' mothers-in-law will stand against their own mothers-in-law.
The man's enemies will be from his own family. (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves a father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. And whoever loves a son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. If anyone loses his life because of me, he will find it.
He who welcomes you welcomes me. And he who welcomes me welcomes him who sent me.
If we assume that someone receives a prophet as a prophet, they will receive the reward of a prophet. And if we assume that someone receives a pious person as a pious person, they will receive the reward of a pious person.
Imagine if someone gave a glass of cold water to a young child following me. What I am going to tell you is the truth. He will certainly be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus completed the education of his twelve disciples. He then moved on to teaching and preaching in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come, or shall we look for someone else?"
Jesus replied, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see."
The blind are given sight. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised to life. And the good news preaches to the poor.
Blessing to those who do not abandon their faith because of me.
While John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? Was it a reed shaken by the wind?"
If it was not, why did you go out to see? A man dressed in elegant clothes? No. People who dress elegantly are in the palaces of kings.
What then did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one spoken of in the Scriptures. It says, "I will send my messenger to prepare the way before you." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is more important than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the Kingdom of Heaven has been forcefully advancing. And those violent men seize it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let he who has ears hear.
To whom do people today most closely resemble? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces, calling out to others. They say:
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not become sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and 'sinners.' "But wisdom is justified by her deeds." Do not move away from sinful cities.
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where he had done most of his miracles. The people there had not turned away from their sins. And he said:
How dreadful it would be for you, Chorazin! How dreadful it would be for you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented of their sins long ago. They would have been clothed in sackcloth. They would have sat in ashes.
But I tell you this: the day of judgment will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And you, Capernaum, what will be your fate? Will you be raised to the heavens? No! But you will descend to the place of the dead. Imagine if the miracles that happened in you had happened in Sodom, it would still be standing today.
But I tell you this: the day of judgment will be easier for Sodom than for you. "Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest."
At that time Jesus said, "I thank you, Father. You are the Lord of heaven and earth. You have hidden these things from the wise and learned, but you have revealed them to the little children."
Yes, Dad. This is what I wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to reveal him to.
Come to me, all of you who are weary and carry heavy burdens, and I will give you rest.
They became my slaves and learned from me. I am gentle and free from pride. You will find rest for your soul.
My service is easy and my burden is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick some of the wheat spikes and eat it.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! This is against the law on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did? He and his men were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Haven't you read the law? It tells us that the priests in the temple are to do their work every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I am telling you that the one who is more important than the temple is here.
The inscription says: "I desire mercy, not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of man is Lord also of the sabbath day.
From there, Jesus entered their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful for us to heal people on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep falls into a well on the Sabbath? Would you not reach in and pull it out?"
A human being is more valuable than a sheep! So the law permits us to do good on the Sabbath.
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, like a new hand, just like the other one.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. The chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew everything about the Pharisees' schemes, so he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed every sick person among them.
But he advised them not to tell anyone who he was.
That was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet Isaiah. He says:
Here is my servant, whom I have chosen, whom I love, and upon whom I am well pleased. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim to the nations that all things shall be well.
He will not argue or shout. His voice will not be heard by anyone in the streets.
What bends a branch, bends a tree. What puts out a flame, puts out a fire. What brings all things to right, brings all things to peace.
The nations shall put their hope in him. (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Pizzybob
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. After that, the man was able to speak and see.
Everyone was amazed. They said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
The Pharisees heard that, and they said, "This man casts out demons by the power of Beelzebul, the prince of demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Every kingdom that fights against itself will be destroyed. And every city or family that is divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that you cast out demons by the power of Beelzebub. By whose power, then, does your people cast out demons? Therefore, they shall be your judges.
But what if the demons were driven out by the Spirit of God? Then the Kingdom of God would have come upon you.
Think about this. How can you get into a strong man's house and simply take what he owns? You have to tie him up first. Then you can rob his house.
He who is not with me is against me. He who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
Here is what I say to you: Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven, but speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
He who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but he who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. No one who blasphemes the Holy Spirit will find forgiveness, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you make a good tree, its fruit will be good. And if you make a bad tree, its fruit will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruit.
O nest of poisonous vipers! How can you, evil one, say anything good? Your mouths speak out of your hearts.
A good man says good things, which spring from the goodness preserved within him. A wicked man says wicked things, which spring from the wickedness preserved within him.
But here is what I tell you. On the day of reckoning, people will be asked about every careless word they spoke.
"In your words, you will be condemned or acquitted." "The miraculous sign of Yunus"
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He answered and said, "Evil and adulterous people seek a sign, but no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise up in the judgment with this generation, and will condemn it; for if the mighty works which were done among them had been done in Sodom, it would have remained to this day. Behold, here is one greater than Jonah is here.
The Queen of the South will rise on the day of judgment with the people who are now living, and she will prove them guilty. She came from a very long way to witness the wisdom of Solomon. And now here is someone more important than Solomon.
What happens when an evil jinn emerges from a human? It wanders through dry areas, searching for a place to rest. But it finds none.
Then he says, "I will return to the house I left." And when he reaches it, he finds the house empty. It has been completely cleaned and tidied.
Then the devil goes away and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than he is. They enter and live there. This man becomes in a worse state than he was before. This is how it will be with the wicked people in our time." The mother and brothers of Jesus
While Jesus was still talking to the crowds, his mother and brothers stood outside. They wanted to talk to him.
She told him personally: "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus replied to him, saying, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples and said, "Here are my mother and my brothers!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On the same day, Jesus left the house and sat on the shore of the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he entered a boat. And he sat in it. Everyone stood on the shore.
Then he told them many stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
Scatter the seeds on the ground. Some fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When dawn broke, the sun scorched the plants. They withered because they had no roots.
Other seeds fell among the thorns; these grew and intertwined to overwhelm the plants.
Other seeds fell on good ground. They yielded fruit thirty, sixty, or a hundredfold, beyond what was sown.
He who has ears, let him hear.
The students came to him and asked him, "Why do you use stories when you address people?"
He answered, saying: "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the kingdom of heaven. They have not been given to those outside."
Everyone who has this kind of knowledge will be given more. In fact, he will receive a lot more. But whoever does not have this kind of knowledge, even what he has little will be taken away from him.
Here's why I use parables when I speak to people. I say, "They look, but they don't really see. They listen, but they don't really hear or understand."
In it the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, "You will hear but not understand, and you will see but not perceive what you are seeing."
The hearts of these people have become callous. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, perhaps they would see with their eyes. Perhaps they would hear with their ears. Perhaps they would understand with their hearts. Perhaps they would turn to the Lord, and then he would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes, for they see; and blessed are your ears, for they hear.
What I am going to tell you now is real. Many prophets and righteous people wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
And others took the seed that fell on rocky places. And they are the ones who hear the good news and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no roots, so they last only for a little while. They quickly fall away from the faith when trouble or persecution comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. They prevent them from bearing fruit.
But others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a harvest that yields 100, 60, and 30 times more than what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus said to the crowd another parable: "Thus is the kingdom of heaven." Then He added, "A man planted good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat started to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's workers came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Then where have the weeds come from?"
"An enemy did this," he answered. "The servants asked him: 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?'"
"No," said its owner. "While you are weeding, you might pull up the wheat along with it."
Let them grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will say to them: First, gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to burn. Then, gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage." "Stories of the mustard seed and the yeast."
Jesus said to the crowd another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took this seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest of all the plants in the garden. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman took some and mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
The words of the Prophet have been fulfilled. He said, "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter things hidden since the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him. They said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the devil.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Resurrection. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are pulled up and burned in the fire. Thus will it be in the day of judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will remove from his kingdom all causes of sin. They will also throw out everyone who does evil.
They will cast them into the burning basin. There they will wail and gnash their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine as a light in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear. The stories of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, went away, and sold everything he had and bought that field.
Again, the Kingdom of Heaven is like a merchant seeking rare pearls.
He found one of great value. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake, and gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled the net to the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish in baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
Thus will the Day of Judgment be. The angels will come. They will separate those who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The evil people will be thrown into the burning oven. There the evil ones will bark and dig with their fangs.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He told them, "Every scribe who is a student of the law knows the kingdoms of heaven as a homeowner. He brings out new treasures from his storehouse, in addition to the old ones." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories, then moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began to teach people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man come from, with this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country, and not in his own house."
He performed some miracles there simply because they were not believers.
John the Baptist's head was cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist, he has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to do miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod Philip's brother.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias' daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He had sworn to her that he would give her what she asked for.
Herodias's daughter said to her mother, "What shall I ask for?" The girl said to Herod, "I want you to give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter."
The king was very angry. But he remembered his promise and his dinner guests. So he told one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod put John the Baptist to death in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowds heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples came to him. They said, "There is nothing here. It is already very late. Send the crowds away. They can go to the villages and buy something to eat."
Jesus replied, "They do not need to go away. Give them something to eat."
"We have five loaves of bread and two fish only," they answered.
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus motioned for the people to sit down on the grass. And He took the five loaves and the two fish. And looking up to heaven, He blessed and broke the loaves. And He gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The students took 12 baskets of leftovers.
About 5000 men who ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately, Jesus instructed the disciples to get into the boat. He told them to go ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he dispersed the crowd.
After dismissing them, he went up alone to the top of a mountain to worship. And when evening came, he was still there alone.
The ship was already far from the shore. The waves were beating it hard because the wind was blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to His disciples; He walked on the surface of the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and they were afraid. "This is a ghost!" they said. And they called out in fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, saying, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
Lord, are you? Peter asked. If it is so, tell me to come to you on the water.
Jesus said, "Come." And Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord, save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. "Your faith is too weak!" He said. "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
Then those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus. And they said, "You are truly the Son of God."
They crossed the lake and reached Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there recognized Jesus. So they sent a message into all the surrounding countryside. And people brought all the sick to Jesus.
They asked him to allow the sick to touch only the edge of his cloak. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. And they asked him,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before eating!
Jesus replied, saying, "And why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Law Code 5:16) He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will surely be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their families: "All the help you have received from us is a gift dedicated to God."
They do not need to honor their parents with this gift. You make the word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
O ye hypocrites! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said:
These people honor me with what they say. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human laws." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowds to Him. He said, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." But what comes out of your mouth, that is what makes you "unclean."
Then his disciples came to him, saying, "Do you not see that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus replied, saying, "There are plants which my Father in heaven did not plant. They will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads another blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Don't you understand yet?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. These are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts proceed from the heart. For murder, adultery, and sexual immorality generally originate here. Likewise, theft, false witness, and lying generally come from the heart.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A woman from Canaan near Tyre and Sidon came to him and called out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! A demon controls my daughter. She suffers greatly."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to Him and requested Him saying, "Send her away, for she cries out after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. "O Lord! Help me!" she said.
He answered, saying: "It is not right to take the bread of the children and throw it to these dogs."
Yes, sir, she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus departed from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to the top of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought to him people who were blind and those who could not walk. They also brought those who were crippled, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak began to speak. The paralyzed were healed. Those who could not walk began to walk. Those who were blind could see. And the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have deep concern for these people. They have been with me for three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will faint on their way home."
His disciples replied to him, saying, "Nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
How many loaves do you have? Jesus asked. "Seven, and a few small fish," they answered.
Jesus told the multitude to sit down on the ground.
The seven loaves and the fish He took, and gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples distributed them to the people.
They all ate and were filled. Afterward, the disciples took seven baskets full of leftovers.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat. He went into the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked about a supernatural sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He replied: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say: 'It will be beautiful. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and overcast." She knows what she sees in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
"A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign miracle. But no sign shall be given to it but the sign of the prophet Jonah." And Jesus left them and departed. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The disciples crossed over to the other side of the lake. They forgot to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus told them. "Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this among themselves. They said: "He must be saying that because we didn't bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you talking to each other about having no bread?"
Don't you still understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Do you not remember the number of baskets in which you gathered the fragments?
Don't you remember the seven loaves for 4000? Don't you remember how many baskets you gathered the fragments from?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was not warning them about the yeast used in bread, but rather was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees taught. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "What do people say about the Son of Man?"
They answered: "Some say John the Baptist, others Elijah, and still others think they see Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus replied, "Blessed are you, Simon, son of Jonah! For no one has revealed this to you, unless it has been given to you from the Holy Spirit."
Here is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to His disciples what was going to happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem. There He would suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes. He would be killed and rise again on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and called Peter, saying, "Depart from me, Satan! You are an offense to me. For you do not savor the things that are of God, but those that are of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "Whoever wants to become my follower must deny themselves, take up their cross, and follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he sacrifices his life for me, he will find a better life.
What good would it do someone to gain the whole world but lose their own soul? Or what could someone possibly give in exchange for their soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father, and His angels will come with Him, and He will reward each person according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the coming of the Son of Man in His kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changed.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John his brother, and led them up onto a high mountain. And He was transfigured before them.
There before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the rays of the sun. His clothing became white as light of day.
At that very moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three tabernacles; one for you, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
While Peter was still talking, a bright cloud enveloped them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my son, whom I love. I am very pleased with him. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly afraid. They fell on their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said, "and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. During the descent, Jesus told them what to do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked, "Why do the Pharisees say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "It is true. Elijah must come first to restore all things.
But I tell you, Elijah has come already. The people did not recognize him. They did to him everything they wanted to do. In the same way, they will make the Son of Man suffer."
Then the disciples realized that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus healed a boy who had a demon.
As they came near the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus; he knelt down before him.
"Sir," he said, "have mercy on my son. He trembles violently and suffers greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they were not able to heal him.
"O people of disbelief and evil!" Jesus answered. "How long must I stay with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to leave the boy, and it departed from him. And he was healed in that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He answered and said, "Because you have so little faith. What I tell you is true. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done. Nothing will be impossible for you."
